NAME
    imagine - put a picture on the screen, with nothing in between

SYNOPSIS
    imagine FILE

DESCRIPTION
    Draws a picture straight onto the display, through /dev/fb0, and
    returns. Nothing else is involved: no window, no canvas, no
    compositor and no palette of its own. What the monitor shows
    afterwards is the decoder's output and the display driver's, and
    nothing of this program's own idea of what the picture should
    look like.

    That is the whole reason it exists. A desktop program draws through
    vgfx, which puts a window on the screen, quantises colour to the
    sixteen palette entries a DAC holds and lets the compositor scale
    the result -- so when a picture comes out wrong there, the decoder,
    the palette and the scaling are all still suspects. This program
    takes the display's own frame instead, and a wrong picture here is
    a wrong picture in px.h or on the hardware.

    The picture is fitted to the display the way a photograph is fitted
    to a page: scaled to fit, centred, keeping its own shape, with the
    rest of the screen black. A 32bpp display gets true colour, a pixel
    being a B8G8R8X8 word written whole. An 8bpp display has a DAC in
    front of it and can hold only one of sixteen slots, so each pixel is
    reduced to the nearest entry of the palette the kernel programmed
    (VNU_SYS_gfx_palette) -- the closest the hardware can get, which is
    the display's limit and not this program's.

    BMP, PNG and JPEG are read, at most 8192 pixels on a side and 4 MiB
    of file. A file that is not one of those, or whose header claims a
    size past those limits, is refused without touching the screen.

    The picture stays on the screen until a key is pressed, which is
    what puts the console back in front of it: the program holds the
    display for exactly as long as it is worth looking at, and gives
    the machine back the moment anyone wants to type at it. Until then
    the frame is the device's to lend and this program's to paint;
    after it the text mode is back, with the console's own characters
    on the screen.

    Where the picture ends up is the device's business, not this
    program's: the desktop draws into the same frame, and it redraws
    when it has a reason to -- a window changed, or the pointer moved --
    so a desktop that is running takes the picture back at its next
    redraw. That is why this one waits for a key rather than going
    straight back to the shell.

FILES
    /dev/fb0            the display's own frame, mapped on open

EXIT STATUS
    0 on success, 1 if the file cannot be read, is not an image this
    can show, or the display cannot be put into a mode with a frame.

THE DEVICE: /dev/fb0

    Opening /dev/fb0 maps the display's frame into the calling
    process at the fixed address VNU_FB_VA and returns a descriptor.
    A read of struct vnu_fb_info (in <vnu/abi.h>) answers where that
    address is and how the frame is laid out: va, width, height, bpp
    (8 or 32), stride in bytes a row, and size for the whole frame.
    The program writes pixels into va directly and then writes zero
    bytes to the descriptor, which puts the frame on the screen. A
    write with data instead copies those bytes into the frame at the
    descriptor's position, which lseek moves. SEEK_END there is the size
    of the frame, one byte past the last row that can be written, so a
    write starting exactly there is refused with EINVAL; ask for the
    tail by a negative offset instead, as in lseek(fd, -stride,
    SEEK_END) for the last row. On the other character devices SEEK_END
    is 0, since a stream has no end.

    Opening the device takes the display with it. A machine at its
    prompt is in text mode, where there is no frame to map, so an
    open there programs the mode the last graphics session left
    behind -- the one the desktop itself would use -- and maps that
    frame. Open fails with ENODEV only when the card cannot be put
    into a mode with a frame at all.

    Closing the last descriptor gives the mode back. The console has
    been sitting in its own cells the whole time, unreadable while the
    card reads a frame, so returning to text mode is what puts those
    characters on the screen again; a desktop that has taken the
    display over in the meantime keeps it. Closing is not what a
    program has to remember to do: the descriptor is recorded against
    the process that opened it, and a process that exits or is killed
    has its descriptors closed for it, so a program that paints and
    then dies hands the machine its prompt back anyway. Until it
    does, what is on the screen stays what was drawn into it.

    Nothing else draws on the display while the descriptor is open.
    The console redraws itself from a scheduler pass, and it waits for
    the close to do it: a pass that ran anyway would paint its
    characters straight over the program's picture, and on a display
    that has no text mode of its own -- a virtio-gpu -- it would hand
    the whole screen back at the console's 720x400 geometry while the
    program still held a frame twice that size. So what is on the
    screen stays what was drawn into it, for as long as the program
    keeps the descriptor open.

    A mode change replaces the frame a descriptor was opened on, so a
    read re-maps and answers with the frame in use now, which may be
    a different size -- read the geometry again rather than trusting
    the one you started with. The frame belongs to the display and not
    to the program: exiting, forking or execveing takes the mapping
    with it and never hands the frame back.

